.TH GXWI-TASK-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-task-manager \- see what is running, and who is signed in, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-task-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Task Manager shows what is running on the machine now, in four views.
Processes lists every process, with its PID, who it runs as, and its share
of the processor and memory. Group by arranges them by service or job, or
by person, and Find narrows them. The process picked is shown in full beside it:
what started it, its command, its service or job, its session, its
integrity, its protection and its mitigations. Performance graphs the last
two minutes of the processors, memory, each disk and each network interface.
Jobs lists the jobs peinit is running. Signed in lists each logon session:
who, how, since when, and its processes.
.PP
It reads /proc as the person looking, and asks peinit which services and
jobs there are and authd who each SID is, with no more authority than they
have. What they may not see is left out or marked, and the window says how
much and why. As shipped, who a process runs as and its command are visible
to that person and to Administrators. A protected process is closed to
everyone, Administrators included, but is still listed. The full list of
sessions is the kernel's, which only Administrators may read; anyone else
sees the sessions their own processes are in.
.PP
End process asks first, then asks the process to end, holding it by a pidfd
so that another given the same PID cannot be ended by mistake; one still
there after five seconds is offered End it now. Stop service and Stop job
ask peinit to stop what the process belongs to. Sign out asks authd to end a
session and every process in it. Each is offered only where the person may:
End process where the process's own descriptor lets them, Stop where peinit
says they may stop it, and Sign out for their own sessions and, as shipped,
anyone's to Administrators. Where they may not, the window says why.
.PP
Everything is read again every two seconds; F5, or Refresh, reads it at
once. Nothing is kept once the window closes. What is defined, rather than
what is running, is Services Manager's.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-task-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Task Manager.
.TP
.I /proc
The processes, and the machine's processor, memory, disk and network
figures.
.SH SEE ALSO
Task Manager, and The Process Security Block, at learn.peios.org.
